D={
"Chinese Revolution":"After eight years fighting Japan and four years of civil war, Mao Zedong's Communists defeated Chiang Kai-shek's Nationalists, who fled to Taiwan. On 1 October 1949 Mao proclaimed the People's Republic of China from Tiananmen. Land reform redistributed land but killed perhaps 1–2 million landlords, and the new state allied with the Soviet Union.",
"Decolonisation of Africa and Asia":"World War II exhausted Britain, France and the Netherlands while nationalist movements grew. India and Pakistan (1947), Burma and Sri Lanka (1948), Indonesia (1949), Ghana (1957) and 17 African countries in 1960 alone won independence. Some struggles were bloody — Algeria (1954–62), Kenya's Mau Mau, Vietnam — and colonial borders often left lasting conflicts.",
"Bangladesh Liberation War":"In 1970 the Awami League under Sheikh Mujibur Rahman won a majority in Pakistan's elections, but West Pakistan's leaders refused to transfer power. On 25 March 1971 the army launched Operation Searchlight; hundreds of thousands to millions were killed and about 10 million fled to India. India intervened in December alongside the Mukti Bahini, and 93,000 Pakistani soldiers surrendered in Dhaka on 16 December.",
"Moon landing":"The Soviet Union launched Sputnik (1957) and Gagarin (1961); President Kennedy then pledged to land Americans on the Moon. On 20 July 1969 Apollo 11's lunar module Eagle landed in the Sea of Tranquility, and Neil Armstrong stepped out saying 'That's one small step for man, one giant leap for mankind.' Six missions landed 12 astronauts by 1972.",
"Fall of the Berlin Wall":"Built in 1961 to stop East Germans fleeing west, the Wall became the symbol of the Cold War. As reform swept Eastern Europe in 1989, an official mistakenly announced on 9 November that borders were open; crowds poured through and began chipping the Wall. Germany reunified on 3 October 1990.",
"Dissolution of the Soviet Union":"Gorbachev's glasnost and perestroika loosened control, and the Baltic states, Ukraine and others pushed for independence. A failed hard-line coup in August 1991 discredited the Communist Party; Boris Yeltsin rose. On 26 December 1991 the Soviet Union dissolved into 15 states.",
"Indian economic liberalisation":"In 1991 India's foreign reserves could cover only weeks of imports, and it pledged gold to borrow. Prime Minister P. V. Narasimha Rao and Finance Minister Manmohan Singh devalued the rupee, cut tariffs, ended most industrial licensing and opened sectors to foreign investment. Growth accelerated, creating India's IT boom and a large middle class.",
"September 11 attacks":"On 11 September 2001, 19 al-Qaeda hijackers seized four airliners, crashing two into New York's World Trade Center and one into the Pentagon; the fourth crashed in Pennsylvania. Nearly 3,000 people died. The US invaded Afghanistan (2001) and Iraq (2003), launching the 'war on terror'.",
"2004 Indian Ocean tsunami":"On 26 December 2004 a magnitude 9.1 undersea earthquake off Sumatra caused waves up to 30 m high. About 230,000 people died in 14 countries, including Indonesia, Sri Lanka, India (Tamil Nadu and the Andaman and Nicobar Islands) and Thailand. It led to an Indian Ocean tsunami warning system.",
"Chandrayaan-3 lands on the Moon":"After Chandrayaan-2's lander crashed in 2019, ISRO launched Chandrayaan-3 on 14 July 2023. Its Vikram lander touched down near the lunar south pole on 23 August 2023, making India the fourth country to soft-land on the Moon and the first near the south pole. The Pragyan rover confirmed sulphur in the lunar soil.",
"Sino-Indian War":"Disputes over the McMahon Line and Aksai Chin, India's 'forward policy' and China's anger over the Dalai Lama's asylum led China to attack on 20 October 1962. Chinese forces advanced in Ladakh and Arunachal Pradesh and then declared a ceasefire, withdrawing in the east. India's defeat led to military modernisation.",
"Cuban Missile Crisis":"In October 1962 US spy planes discovered Soviet nuclear missiles in Cuba. Kennedy imposed a naval 'quarantine', and the world came closer to nuclear war than ever before. Khrushchev withdrew the missiles in exchange for a US pledge not to invade Cuba and the secret removal of US missiles from Turkey.",
"Green Revolution in India":"After droughts in 1965–66 forced India to import food, scientists M. S. Swaminathan and Norman Borlaug introduced high-yield dwarf wheat and rice. With irrigation, fertilisers and support prices, Punjab and Haryana's output soared and India became self-sufficient. It also caused groundwater depletion and soil problems.",
"Emergency in India":"After a court voided her election and opposition protests grew, Prime Minister Indira Gandhi declared a state of emergency on 25 June 1975. Opposition leaders were jailed, the press censored, and forced sterilisation campaigns were run. She lost the 1977 election to the Janata Party.",
"Iranian Revolution":"Shah Mohammad Reza Pahlavi's autocratic modernisation and secret police (SAVAK) provoked protests by clerics, students and workers. The Shah fled in January 1979, and Ayatollah Khomeini returned from exile to found an Islamic Republic. US embassy staff were held hostage for 444 days.",
"Bhopal gas tragedy":"On the night of 2–3 December 1984 about 40 tonnes of methyl isocyanate leaked from Union Carbide's pesticide plant in Bhopal. Thousands died within days and estimates of total deaths reach 15,000–20,000, with hundreds of thousands injured. It is the world's worst industrial disaster.",
"Chernobyl disaster":"On 26 April 1986 a safety test went wrong at Reactor 4 of the Chernobyl plant in Soviet Ukraine, causing an explosion and fire. Radioactive fallout spread over Europe, 350,000 people were relocated, and an exclusion zone remains. Soviet secrecy damaged trust and helped glasnost.",
"End of apartheid":"Apartheid, enforced from 1948, segregated South Africa by race. Under internal resistance and international sanctions, President F. W. de Klerk released Nelson Mandela in 1990. Negotiations led to the first democratic elections in April 1994, and Mandela became president.",
"Rwandan genocide":"After the president's plane was shot down on 6 April 1994, Hutu extremist militias and soldiers killed about 800,000 Tutsi and moderate Hutu in about 100 days, often with machetes, encouraged by radio broadcasts. The UN failed to intervene. The Tutsi-led RPF ended the genocide in July.",
"2008 financial crisis":"Risky US mortgage lending and complex securities created a housing bubble. When it burst, Lehman Brothers collapsed in September 2008, freezing credit worldwide. Governments bailed out banks and the global economy fell into the Great Recession.",
"Arab Spring":"In December 2010 the self-immolation of Tunisian street vendor Mohamed Bouazizi sparked protests that toppled Tunisia's Ben Ali. Uprisings spread to Egypt, Libya, Yemen, Bahrain and Syria. Tunisia briefly democratised, Egypt returned to military rule, and Syria, Libya and Yemen fell into civil wars.",
"India becomes most populous country":"In April 2023, according to UN estimates, India overtook China with about 1.43 billion people, while China's population had begun to decline. India's population is young, with a median age under 30. The shift has implications for jobs, resources and global influence.",
"Great Pyramid built":"The Great Pyramid of Giza was built for the Fourth Dynasty king Khufu around 2560 BCE, about 146 m tall from around 2.3 million blocks. Workers were paid labourers housed in a nearby village, and the Wadi al-Jarf papyri, the oldest inscribed papyri, record a team transporting limestone. It was the tallest human-made structure for about 3,800 years.",
"Thera eruption":"The Minoan eruption of Thera (Santorini) around 1600 BCE was one of the largest in the past 10,000 years, burying Akrotiri under metres of ash. It produced tsunamis that struck Crete. Its exact date is debated, with radiocarbon and archaeology pointing to slightly different times.",
}
